{"arguments": [
 {"id": "G7", "name": "Future-unknown appeals cannot defeat miracles", "conclusion": "The skeptical appeal to a future unknown natural explanation is self-defeating and cannot rationally defeat a miracle claim.", "speaker": "Gary Habermas", "premises": ["P1 If one can always say 'maybe we will discover a natural explanation in the future,' the same move can be made against any disliked claim, such as the moon landing or planetary astronomy.", "P2 The proper comeback is that maybe it will not happen in the future.", "P3 Naturalism itself is often a non-empirical claim, so empiricist skeptics who use it inconsistently flunk their own epistemology.", "P4 Habermas argues against seventeen or eighteen such Humean empirical comebacks in volume 2."], "strength": {"st1": {"v": 2, "quote": "And the comeback, the right comeback is, and maybe it won't happen in the future."}, "st2": {"v": 2, "quote": "you can say it about anything you don't like well maybe it'll be done in the future"}, "st3": {"v": 1, "quote": "In volume two, I have a lot of pages, two chapters divi devoted to David Hume."}, "st4": {"v": 1, "quote": "If naturalism is not an empirical claim, but your epistemology requires empirical claims. Guess what? You flunked."}, "st5": {"v": 1, "quote": "Well, maybe find an answer in the future. Oh, that's brilliant there, man."}, "st6": {"v": 1, "quote": "you can say it about anything you don't like well maybe it'll be done in the future"}, "st7": {"v": 1, "quote": "If we're in the category of yes, no, yes, no. Then we did it."}, "st8": {"v": 1, "quote": "one of which is it just makes induction impossible."}}, "prior_art": "David Hume, Of Miracles (An Enquiry Concerning Human Understanding, 1748); Habermas, The Resurrection, vol. 2 (2022)", "originality": {"or1": {"v": 1, "quote": "I argue against I think it's 17 or 18 of them in the second Hume chapter in in volume two.", "why": "A variant: it inverts Hume's future-evidence move rather than restating Hume's argument."}, "or2": {"v": 1, "quote": "What if I find out in the future we didn't go to the moon?"}, "or3": {"v": 1, "quote": "If naturalism is not an empirical claim, but your epistemology requires empirical claims.", "why": "Connects philosophy of science (induction, empiricism) to the miracle debate."}, "or4": {"v": 0, "quote": "", "why": "No formalization or model offered."}}, "weakest_link": "P2 ('maybe it won't happen') is a bare counter-assertion that by itself does not settle the evidential question.", "develop_next": "Give a criterion for when a future-unknown appeal is legitimate vs. ad hoc, rather than a symmetric 'maybe it won't.'", "case_map": ["K16"], "domain": "philosophy"},
 {"id": "G8", "name": "Supernatural events can be linked to God rather than Satan", "conclusion": "If a skeptic grants a supernatural event, linking it to God rather than Satan is reasonable because the skeptic's own worldview cannot accommodate Satan or other supernatural beings.", "speaker": "Gary Habermas", "premises": ["P1 The skeptic who says 'maybe Satan did it' has already admitted supernatural beings into his theory, which he normally denies.", "P2 If supernatural beings, intelligent design, fine-tuning, an afterlife, and objective ethics are granted, all five require a being fairly called God.", "P3 Therefore the skeptic who grants the supernatural has sped up the case for God rather than avoided it."], "strength": {"st1": {"v": 2, "quote": "Maybe Satan did it. Uh excuse me, but last time I checked, you don't allow Satan anywhere in your theory."}, "st2": {"v": 2, "quote": "If you have supernatural beings, if you have intelligent design, if you have fine-tuning, if you have an afterlife, if you have objective ethics, um, from, you know, out of nowhere, uh, causally, uh, I think all five of those require, uh, a being that that's fair to call him God."}, "st3": {"v": 0, "quote": ""}, "st4": {"v": 1, "quote": "Now, we've got about five things here that seem that they require God."}, "st5": {"v": 1, "quote": "I don't know. Now, we've got about five things here that seem that they require God."}, "st6": {"v": 0, "quote": "I think all five of those require, uh, a being that that's fair to call him God."}, "st7": {"v": 0, "quote": ""}, "st8": {"v": 1, "quote": "If intelligent design is True. That may not prove God, but it sure helps."}}, "weakest_link": "P2 asserts all five items 'require' God without argument for any of them.", "develop_next": "Argue at least one of the five (e.g. objective ethics) to God rather than asserting the bundle.", "case_map": ["K07", "K11"], "domain": "philosophy"},
 {"id": "G9", "name": "Competing miracle claims do not match the resurrection", "conclusion": "Competing miracle claims from other religions or Christian traditions do not undermine the resurrection because they lack comparable evidence and often do not require God.", "speaker": "Gary Habermas", "premises": ["P1 One must investigate each alleged competing miracle, and most cannot be verified even by their own adherents.", "P2 Alleged levitation or anti-gravity events do not require God and are not first-rate miracles.", "P3 The Quran has no verse that Muhammad did miracles, but it does have verses that Jesus did miracles.", "P4 Marian apparitions such as Medjugorje or Zeitoun are contested, often involve only a handful of visionaries, and are usually within the Christian fold.", "P5 The resurrection is the culmination of Jesus' unique life, teaching, predictions, and ethical influence, so it stands alone."], "strength": {"st1": {"v": 2, "quote": "you'd have to prove each of those miracles. And again, I don't think even Christians can get away making that kind of claim."}, "st2": {"v": 2, "quote": "you can't give me a verse in the Quran that that Muhammad did miracles, but there's a verse in the Quran that Jesus did miracles."}, "st3": {"v": 1, "quote": "in Medjugoria the people watch the kids watch Mary. There's only a handful of them."}, "st4": {"v": 1, "quote": "I don't think this their competing miracles help because you'd have to prove each of those miracles."}, "st5": {"v": 1, "quote": "I want to know what these competing miracle claims are. I want to know if you want to get right down to it."}, "st6": {"v": 0, "quote": "I think it's unique when it's unique with Jesus."}, "st7": {"v": 1, "quote": "We're never going to get to the end of this because we have to investigate each one and be sure it's true."}, "st8": {"v": 2, "quote": "you don't find this stuff outside of the uh I I mean true true cases"}}, "weakest_link": "P5 (resurrection as unique culmination) assumes the uniqueness it needs to show.", "develop_next": "Give explicit, side-by-side evidential criteria and score the resurrection against two named rival miracle claims.", "case_map": ["K15", "K16"], "domain": "history"},
 {"id": "G10", "name": "Arguments for God and from miracles work both ways", "conclusion": "One can argue prospectively from God to the resurrection or retrospectively from the resurrection back to God, and Scripture does both.", "speaker": "Gary Habermas", "premises": ["P1 The prospective path moves from God to Jesus and his resurrection.", "P2 The retrospective path begins with the unique event and asks who could have done it, then connects to arguments for God.", "P3 The New Testament argues both ways, so the direction is not a problem."], "strength": {"st1": {"v": 2, "quote": "I think not only it works both ways or can I think in scripture it works both ways."}, "st2": {"v": 2, "quote": "arguing for God to Jesus and his resurrection I call prospective. Once you have God you move forward to the resurrection."}, "st3": {"v": 0, "quote": ""}, "st4": {"v": 1, "quote": "I think the New Testament argues both ways. I I just I don't have an I don't have an issue with it."}, "st5": {"v": 0, "quote": ""}, "st6": {"v": 1, "quote": "I think you can do it both ways"}, "st7": {"v": 0, "quote": ""}, "st8": {"v": 1, "quote": "Then the other one I call retrospective and that's oh wow look how unique this event is."}}, "weakest_link": "P3 ('Scripture does both') is asserted with no cited texts.", "develop_next": "Cite the specific NT passages that argue each direction and show the two directions are not circular.", "case_map": ["K13", "K16"], "domain": "theology"},
 {"id": "G11", "name": "Chapter one offers considerations, not strict proofs, for God", "conclusion": "Chapter one does not attempt to prove God's existence deductively but presents a theistic universe with good considerations and signs.", "speaker": "Gary Habermas", "premises": ["P1 Habermas calls it a theistic universe rather than proofs for God.", "P2 He includes epistemic truths, morality, fine-tuning, intelligent design, and the afterlife as good extensions.", "P3 He does not think many things can be proved apodictically; things are established by high probability."], "strength": {"st1": {"v": 2, "quote": "I don't argue that I don't try to prove God's existence in chapter one. What I'm saying there is I call it a theistic universe, not proofs for God."}, "st2": {"v": 2, "quote": "EP epis epistemic truths, how do we know anything? I mentioned morality even though I didn't do it. Fine-tuning, intelligent design, uh afterlife."}, "st3": {"v": 1, "quote": "I think things are established by high probability, not by any chance to lay down some, you know, proof that nobody can argue with."}, "st4": {"v": 2, "quote": "I wasn't trying to quote unquote prove God's existence."}, "st5": {"v": 0, "quote": ""}, "st6": {"v": 2, "quote": "I gave um a number of I think really good signs."}, "st7": {"v": 0, "quote": ""}, "st8": {"v": 1, "quote": "I think those are really good extensions"}}, "weakest_link": "P3's 'high probability' standard is never given a threshold or method.", "develop_next": "State the probabilistic standard and show the theistic considerations meet it while rivals do not.", "case_map": ["K01", "K11"], "domain": "philosophy"},
 {"id": "G12", "name": "Jesus claimed deity, especially in Mark 2 and Mark 14", "conclusion": "Jesus claimed to be deity through a multiplicity of texts, with Mark 14:61-64 being the strongest.", "speaker": "Gary Habermas", "premises": ["P1 Critics who demand a verse where Jesus says 'I am God' are misguided because Jesus never used that exact phrase.", "P2 In Mark 2, Jesus forgives sins and heals to show he has authority on earth to forgive sins, which the rabbis recognized as God's prerogative.", "P3 In Mark 14, Jesus says 'I am,' claims to be the cloud rider, and says he will sit at the right hand of God, which the high priest calls blasphemy.", "P4 Del Rosario's dissertation on Mark 2 and Mark 14 provides the best treatment of these claims and answers to objections."], "strength": {"st1": {"v": 2, "quote": "Probably I think the best text for Jesus claiming to be deity is Mark 14:61-64."}, "st2": {"v": 2, "quote": "The big one is what he says last. I will be seated on the right hand of God."}, "st3": {"v": 2, "quote": "the high priest immediately tears his garment and says you committed blasphemy"}, "st4": {"v": 1, "quote": "in the Old Testament, only God is a cloud rider."}, "st5": {"v": 2, "quote": "Now, some critics, Bartman's a good example, some critics will say, I I challenge you Christians. Give me one verse where Jesus says, \"I am God.\""}, "st6": {"v": 1, "quote": "I think the fact that he claimed to be deity or divinity is shown by a multiplicity of things."}, "st7": {"v": 0, "quote": ""}, "st8": {"v": 2, "quote": "First one is one we already did. the guy who comes through the roof and he says, \"Son, your sins are forgiven.\""}}, "weakest_link": "P3's cloud-rider and right-hand-seat claims depend on contested exegesis of Daniel 7 and Psalm 110.", "develop_next": "Lay out Del Rosario's criteria for a deity claim and rebut the 'son of man = mere human' reading explicitly.", "case_map": ["K16"], "domain": "history"}
]}

